[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Provider and ChangeNotifier Deep Dive in Flutter"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"State management is one of the most crucial concepts in Flutter development. Among the various solutions available, the combination of Provider and ChangeNotifier remains an official recommendation by the Flutter team for small to medium-sized applications due to its simplicity, performance, and scalability."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding ChangeNotifier"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"ChangeNotifier is a simple class included in the Flutter foundation package. It provides change notification to its listeners. When you design a model class, you can extend ChangeNotifier to encapsulate the application state and business logic."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class CounterModel extends ChangeNotifier {
  int _count = 0;

  int get count => _count;

  void increment() {
    _count++;
    notifyListeners();
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Concepts of ChangeNotifier"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Encapsulation: State variables are kept private (like _count) to prevent unauthorized mutations from outside the model."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Getters: Read-only getters expose the state safely to the UI widgets."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"notifyListeners(): This method must be called whenever the state changes so that all subscribed UI components trigger a rebuild."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating with Provider"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once your ChangeNotifier is created, you need a way to inject it into the widget tree. The Provider package offers ChangeNotifierProvider, which creates an instance of a ChangeNotifier, exposes it down the tree, and automatically disposes of it when it is no longer needed."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"void main() {
  runApp(
    ChangeNotifierProvider(
      create: (context) => CounterModel(),
      child: const MyApp(),
    ),
  );
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Consuming the State"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are primarily three ways to read and listen to data from a Provider inside your widgets: Provider.of(), Consumer, and context.watch(). Choosing the right approach helps optimize performance."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"class CounterScreen extends StatelessWidget {
  const CounterScreen({Key? key}) : super(key: key);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    final counter = context.watch<CounterModel>();

    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Provider Deep Dive')),
      body: Center(
        child: Text('Count: \${counter.count}', style: const TextStyle(fontSize: 24)),
      ),
      floatingActionButton: FloatingActionButton(
        onPressed: () => context.read<CounterModel>().increment(),
        child: const Icon(Icons.add),
      ),
    );
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Performance Optimization Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use context.read() inside callbacks, event handlers, and methods where you only need to invoke a function without listening to state changes."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use context.watch() at the top of the build method only when the entire widget needs to rebuild upon state changes."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the Consumer widget to wrap only the specific subtree that needs rebuilding, preventing the unnecessary rebuild of parent widgets."}]
